  "sections": [
    {
      "heading": "This is a power ranking, not a ballot",
      "body": "The first Evercraft College Football Power Rankings deliberately do not start with the AP poll and shuffle a few teams for flavor. CFBPI v0.2 begins with completed NCAA game evidence, builds an iterative opponent-strength rating, caps scoring margins so one blowout cannot own a season, adjusts for designated venue, gives recent form a bounded role and leaves unsupported inputs missing instead of quietly inventing them. The result is a ranking of who has demonstrated the strongest current profile in our model, not a forecast of playoff selection and not a claim that undefeated automatically means better."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Why Alabama is No. 1",
      "body": "Alabama is the clearest separation in this edition. The Crimson Tide are 5-0 with an 81.49 Power Score, more than seven points clear of Notre Dame at No. 2. Their profile combines an 80.8 result-quality component, 91.6 opponent-adjusted efficiency and 62.2 schedule strength. The 56-23 win over Mississippi State is the model's strongest Alabama result so far. The AP poll currently has Alabama sixth, which makes this our first major disagreement with consensus and gives the ranking an immediate test: if Alabama's underlying strength is real, the next stretch should make that gap easier to defend rather than harder."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Where Evercraft breaks hardest from the AP",
      "body": "The interesting part is not that our top teams mostly look familiar. It is where the ordering refuses to follow reputation. Texas Tech is No. 4 here versus No. 11 in the AP poll. UCLA is No. 6 versus No. 21. Pittsburgh is No. 13 versus No. 25, while Kentucky, Wisconsin, Northwestern, Baylor and James Madison make our Top 25 without appearing in the AP Top 25. The other direction matters too: LSU is No. 20 here versus No. 10 in the AP poll, Florida is No. 24 versus No. 16, and Oregon sits just outside our Top 25 at No. 26 despite being No. 13 in the AP poll. Those are not bugs to hide. They are the hypotheses this franchise now has to defend with future results."
    },
    {
      "heading": "The model is not pretending to know everything",
      "body": "This edition has real blind spots and labels them. Player availability is not yet included because we do not have a governed injury and quarterback-status feed wired into the ranking cycle. Turnover sustainability is also left missing rather than estimated from incomplete data. NCAA's current scoreboard feed provides designated home and away teams but does not reliably expose neutral-site state for every game, so the venue layer is conservative. Those missing components reduce confidence instead of becoming fake zeroes. That is less dramatic than pretending the model sees everything, but it makes the historical record much more useful."
    },
    {
      "heading": "We are keeping score on ourselves",
      "body": "This edition is frozen. The ranking run ID, model version, Top 25, component scores, upstream NCAA query definition, ledger digest and AP comparison are published with the story as machine-readable evidence. Next week's edition will be a new receipt, not a rewrite of this one. That gives us a scoreboard for the scoreboard: which teams rose before consensus caught up, which outliers collapsed, whether our biggest disagreements predicted anything useful, and whether model changes actually improve future performance. If the ranking is going to become an Evercraft media property, it should earn credibility in public instead of borrowing it from a logo."
    }
  ],
